# Lean Terminal

A conviction-based swing trading terminal that translates 18 mathematical methods into one sentence a driver would understand.

> "I had no coding experience when I started thinking about this. About six months later, this terminal runs my real money."

---

## The Story

Late August 2025. I screenshot my portfolio and ask, "how can I improve this?" I have no programming background, no formal finance training, no quantitative methods education. What I have is an opinion that markets have patterns, and a stubborn belief I can find them.

Six months and a dozen build sessions later, this is what exists:

- **7,777 lines** of self-contained HTML running in any browser
- **18 mathematical methods** spanning information theory, dynamical systems, stochastic processes, and signal analysis
- **6 tabs** for analysis, comparison, regime detection, and historical backtest
- **Live deployment** at [nomnomsoes.github.io/lean-terminal](https://nomnomsoes.github.io/lean-terminal)
- **Real capital** deployed daily through this system

This is an AI-assisted project. Every concept, architecture decision, signal design, and product call is mine. The implementation was built collaboratively. In 2026 this is industry standard — what matters is whether you understand what you built and whether it works.

---

## Timeline

The project evolved through distinct phases:

- **Aug 2025** — Began screenshot-based portfolio research. Asking "how do I improve this?"
- **Oct 2025** — Named the campaign approach "Lean." Started thinking systematically.
- **Nov 2025** — Refined to "Lean 2.0" with risk-tier framework and disciplined sizing.
- **Feb 2026** — First "Lean Terminal" concept as an iPhone-readable app.
- **Mar 2026** — Heavy build phase. 600+ message sessions wiring the engine.
- **Apr 2026** — Formal L sessions begin (L1 on April 10). System goes live.
- **Today** — L12. 15 logged trades, 66 shipped features, 7,777 lines.

The "8-month journey" includes everything from first portfolio screenshot to today. The actual coded Lean Terminal as we know it took form over the past several months. The formal operator phase — where I trade based on the system's signals — has only been running about three weeks.

---

## The Approach

Most trading dashboards show you data and ask you to interpret it. This one shows you data, runs eighteen interpretations of it, then synthesizes those interpretations into a single actionable sentence.

The most distinctive feature is the **bus analogy** — translating 18 distinct mathematical methods into a single ten-state UX layer:

```
🚌 BUS AT THE STOP — HOP ON NOW
   8 EMA just crossed above 20 EMA today

🚌 on the bus — stay seated
   next stop around $204.30 (2.3% from 20 EMA)

🚌 next stop — get ready
   8 EMA slipping below 20, exit setup forming

🚌 GET OFF — HOP OFF NOW
   8 EMA just crossed below 20 EMA today
```

This isn't gimmickry. It's a translation layer between mathematical sophistication and actionable clarity. Behind the bus state is the full mathematical stack — but on the surface, it's a sentence anyone can understand.

---

## The Math

The terminal runs eighteen methods on every ticker click:

**Trend & Momentum**
- 8 / 20 / 50 EMA stack with crossover detection
- Adaptive trailing stop (EMA-based)
- Runner strategy (75% exit, 25% hold)

**Cycles & Frequency**
- Fourier cycle detection
- Cycle position tracking (0–100% phase)
- Signal decay half-life

**Stochastic Processes**
- Markov state probabilities (UP / FLAT / DOWN with continuation rates)
- Fokker-Planck probability density
- Monte Carlo path projections

**Information Theory**
- Shannon entropy (noise score)
- Lyapunov exponent (chaos vs stability)
- Confidence-weighted ensemble scoring

**Risk & Stress**
- Heat equation diffusion (cross-asset stress map)
- Pearson correlation (portfolio X-ray)
- Fatigue scoring (cumulative stress)
- Drawdown circuit breaker (10% peak-to-trough)

**Composite**
- Bus state classifier (10 states)
- Lens 5-layer regime overlay
- Mini lens compact verdict
- Per-ticker signal trust scoring

---

## The Six Tabs

**Terminal** — Daily decision dashboard. Today's moves, action engine, system metrics, top bull / top bear / highest reaction.

**DNA Lab** — Deep analysis on individual tickers. Full DNA panel, candlestick charts, history breakdown.

**Cheat Sheet** — Quick reference for what every score means and how to read every signal.

**Monte / Markov** — Probabilistic modeling. Monte Carlo path projections, Markov state flow grid, transition matrices.

**Race Card** — Comparative ranking with full math context. Used for "which of these should I trade?" decisions.

**Lens** — 5-layer regime overlay. Stock layer, family layer, market layer, calendar layer, anomaly layer. The verdict.

---

## The Baseball Card

Click any ticker on any tab and a baseball card opens — a single screen showing every number that matters:

- Holding status, P&L, theme classification, ranking
- Verdict line (synthesized natural language)
- Bus state (current action — hop on, stay seated, hop off)
- Signal strength, signal trust, system call, position size
- Direction probabilities (Markov: % up / flat / down)
- 8 / 20 / 50 EMA stack with current price relationship
- EMA stack state classifier (full bull / fading / weak / full bear / mixed)
- Adaptive stop level
- Noise, fatigue, runner allocation
- Mini lens (5 dots — stock / family / market / calendar / anomaly)
- "Does this fit?" overlap analysis for non-held tickers
- Buttons: compare, lens, full DNA, race card, close

No scrolling. No menus. No tabs to navigate. Click a ticker, get the full picture.

---

## Philosophy — Tools, Not Outputs

This terminal was always meant to be a **tool set to accomplish the job of decision making**. Not a black box. Not an oracle. Not a buy-or-sell signal generator that tells you what to do.

A tool is something you pick up, point at a question, and use to think more clearly. A precise tool creates precise products. A precise decision-making tool creates precise decisions.

That distinction shows up everywhere in how the system is built:

- **The bus state** does not say "buy now." It tells you the trend's structural state, in language a driver would understand. You decide whether to act.
- **The lens** does not say "this trade is wrong." It tells you how many of the five layers — stock, family, market, calendar, anomaly — agree right now. You weigh that against your conviction.
- **The X-Ray** does not say "your portfolio is bad." It shows you which of your positions are the same bet held twice and which are diversifiers. You choose what to do with that.
- **The Monte Carlo / Markov** panels do not say "this stock will hit $300." They show you the probability distribution of where it could land in 30 days. You decide whether the asymmetry is worth the position size.

Every panel is an instrument. The user is the operator.

### Precision is constantly tested

The math methods are not chosen for sophistication. They are chosen for precision — for the specific job each one does:

- Fourier for cycle detection because cycles are periodic.
- Markov chains for probability flow because state transitions are state-dependent.
- Fokker-Planck for density estimation because price action diffuses.
- Lyapunov for chaos detection because we need to know when the system is unpredictable.
- Heat equation diffusion for stress propagation because pressure spreads.

When a method stops earning its place — when its signal doesn't translate into better decisions — it gets retired. The Mandelbrot set fractal explorer never made it into production for this reason. Neither did several oscillator indicators that looked sophisticated but added noise. The dock catches every idea. Most never ship. The discipline is at the build step, not the curiosity step.

This is also why the loading dock exists at all. Every feature gets logged before it gets built, every feature gets validated against the question "does this make decisions more precise?" — and most candidates die in the dock without consuming code.

### What this means for the user

If you treat this terminal like a slot machine — pull the lever, look at the score, push buy — you will lose money. The system was not designed for that workflow.

If you treat it as a research instrument — point it at a hypothesis, read what the math says, weigh against your own conviction, decide with discipline — it earns its place in your daily workflow.

The bus line, the lens, the score, the stack — these are not the conclusions of the system. They are the language the system uses to talk to you. The conclusion is the trade you decide to make, or the trade you decide to bench.

The job of the system is to make that conversation clearer. The job of the operator is to listen.

---

## How to Read a Ticker — The Decision Path

Most days, reading a ticker should take 30 seconds. The system is built so the quick-look indicators handle 80% of decisions, and the deeper tools are reserved for the 20% where you genuinely need more information.

The right path is not to use every tool every time. It's to trust the quick-look tools when they agree, and only dig deeper when they disagree or when you're choosing between alternatives.

### Step 1 — The Bus Line (3 seconds)

This is the first read, every time, no exceptions. Click the ticker. The bus line at the top of the baseball card tells you what to do.

- 🚌 *BUS AT THE STOP — HOP ON NOW* → strong action signal, dig deeper
- 🚌 *on the bus — stay seated* → already a good situation, no urgency
- 🚌 *next stop — get ready* → exit setup forming, watch
- 🚌 *GET OFF — HOP OFF NOW* → strong exit signal, dig deeper
- 🚌 *no bus in sight* / *bus left* → skip, move on

If the bus is calm and you already hold the position, you're done. Close the card.

### Step 2 — The Mini Lens (3 seconds)

Five dots showing whether five independent layers agree:

- **5/5 or 4/5** → the picture is clear, signals agree
- **3/5** → mixed, proceed with caution
- **2/5 or fewer** → blurry, the bus might be lying

This is the sanity check on the bus. If they agree, high conviction. If they disagree, you have a decision to make.

### Step 3 — Signal Strength + System Call (5 seconds)

The four big numbers at the top of the card.

- **Score 90+ with BUY call** → high quality
- **Score 75-89 with BUY call** → solid
- **Score below 75** → weak, even if bus says go
- **Signal Trust significantly lower than Signal Strength** → the system is unsure of itself

Most days, you stop here. Three green lights (bus, lens, score) means trade. One green light means bench.

### Step 4 — The Stack and Direction Bar (10 seconds)

Confirmation or contradiction.

- **Stack ▲▲▲ full bull** → strong trend confirmation
- **Direction bar 60%+ up** → probability supports the move
- **Stack ▲▽▽ or ▽▲▲** → mixed, trend unclear

If the stack agrees with the bus and lens, you're done. If it disagrees, continue.

### Step 5 — Risk Panel (5 seconds, only when buying)

Before clicking execute:

- **Stop level** → are you OK with that loss size?
- **Noise above 80** → signals are unreliable today, consider waiting
- **Fatigue above 70** → trend is exhausted, late entry

Healthy ranges → execute. One concern → reduce size by 50%. Two concerns → bench.

### Step 6 — Deep Tools (only when needed)

These tools answer specific questions. You shouldn't need them every day.

| Question | Tool |
|----------|------|
| "Should I pick NVDA or SMH today?" | Compare Tray |
| "Is my whole portfolio safe?" | Portfolio X-Ray |
| "Is the regime saying yes?" | Lens Tab (full) |
| "Where could this go in 30 days?" | Monte / Markov |
| "Where is this in its cycle?" | DNA Lab + cycle position |
| "What's the historical pattern here?" | Race Card |

If you find yourself spending 5+ minutes on a ticker, the system is telling you it's not a clear setup. That's information. Bench it.

### The Daily 30-Second Routine

```
1. Open terminal at 4:15 PM PT
2. Glance at Today's Moves panel
3. For each flagged ticker:
   - Read bus line (3 sec)
   - Read mini lens (3 sec)
   - Read score (3 sec)
   - Decide: trade, bench, or dig deeper
4. Execute in Robinhood
5. Log trade
```

When the math reduces to a single sentence and a row of dots, it can feel like something must be missing. That feeling is the system working correctly. The complexity is still there — it just already happened, in the bus line you're reading.

---

## The Stack

- **Frontend:** Single-file HTML, no build step, no framework
- **Data layer:** Google Sheets CSV publish feeds (read-only)
- **Live data:** Alpaca market data API
- **Hosting:** GitHub Pages (free, fast, version-controlled)
- **State:** localStorage for user preferences and settings
- **Math:** Pure vanilla JavaScript, no external libraries

This is intentional. The system can be deployed anywhere, runs without a backend, has zero dependencies, and survives indefinitely. The entire trading system is one HTML file.

---

## Live Operation

The terminal connects to:

- **Google Sheets** — for ranking, holdings, price history, Markov current state, and macro indicators
- **Alpaca** — for live price feeds and execution (paper / live toggle)
- **Robinhood** — for manual trade execution

The flow:

1. Google Sheet computes scores, signals, and probabilities (server side via formulas)
2. Terminal pulls CSV feeds, runs 18 math methods locally
3. User reviews baseball card and bus state
4. Trade executes through Robinhood (manual) or Alpaca (paper)
5. Result logs to TRADE_LOG, BEHAVIOR_LOG, and HISTORY tabs

---

## The Discipline

This system is operated by rules:

- **Daily routine:** open terminal at 4:15 PM PT, log portfolio value to HISTORY, log any trades to TRADE_LOG, log any overrides to BEHAVIOR_LOG.
- **Circuit breaker:** if portfolio drops 10% from peak, sell all and wait 14 days. Non-negotiable.
- **Bench days are valid:** if signals are noisy, do nothing. Logged just like trade days.
- **Behavior log is mandatory:** every system override gets a written reason. No exceptions.

The discipline is the system. The math is just the input.

